            "<strong>Exclusive breastfeeding</strong> for six months and continued breastfeeding to two years and beyond is the foundation; its support was covered with newborn care and is repeated at every child welfare visit. At <strong>six months</strong> breast milk alone no longer meets the child's needs and <strong>complementary feeding</strong> begins — not at three months because the baby 'is big', not at nine because 'he has no teeth'. The counselling points are remembered as frequency, amount, thickness, variety, responsive feeding and hygiene. <strong>Frequency</strong>: two to three meals a day at 6 to 8 months, three to four at 9 to 11 and 12 to 23 months, plus one to two nutritious snacks, with breastfeeding on demand throughout. <strong>Amount</strong>: start with two to three spoonfuls and build to half a 250-millilitre cup per meal by 8 months, about half a cup at 9 to 11 months, and three-quarters to a full cup from 12 months. <strong>Thickness</strong>: the porridge must be thick enough for a spoon to stand in it — a watery koko fills the stomach with water and carries almost no energy. <strong>Variety</strong>: every day something from each group — the staple, a legume or animal food (egg, fish powder, beans, groundnut paste, the small dried fish eaten whole with their bones), a vitamin-A-rich fruit or vegetable (mango, pawpaw, kontomire, orange-fleshed sweet potato, red palm oil) — mashed at first, finely chopped and finger foods from nine months, family foods from twelve. The <strong>enriched porridge</strong> is the practical lesson: a cereal–legume blend such as Weanimix (roughly three to four parts maize to one part soya or cowpea with some roasted groundnut) cooked thick, with a spoon of palm oil or groundnut paste, an egg, fish powder or mashed beans stirred in, and a vitamin-A-rich vegetable — demonstrated at the clinic and in mother-to-mother groups with the mothers cooking, not watching.",
