            "Acute care is organised around the visit; <strong>chronic care</strong> is organised around the year. Its elements are a <strong>register</strong> of every person with hypertension, diabetes, epilepsy, sickle cell disease and asthma in the zone, with a record card for each; an <strong>appointment system</strong> with monthly or quarterly review dates that are written down and reminded by the volunteer; <strong>refills</strong> that do not cost a day's trading — multi-month dispensing where the protocol allows, a community refill point, the volunteer who collects for the housebound; <strong>defaulter tracing</strong> within the week a review is missed; <strong>self-management support</strong> — the home blood pressure monitor shared by a group, the glucose diary, the foot inspection, the inhaler technique, the seizure diary, the sick-day rules; <strong>peer support groups</strong> of people with the same condition who meet monthly, measure one another's pressure, cook together and bring back the ones who stopped; the <strong>family</strong>, who buy and cook the food and notice the symptoms; and a <strong>referral and back-referral</strong> link with the health centre or hospital clinician who initiates and adjusts treatment, so that the person is seen near home between the specialist visits. Trained community health nurses screen, counsel, refill under the clinician's plan, and refer; the protocol says what they may adjust and what they must refer.",
